1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,359
In 2023, a New York lawyer

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,119
gave a judge a list of past

3
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:04,820
court cases.

4
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:06,660
The judge looked them up.

5
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:08,100
They didn't exist.

6
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,560
ChatGPT had made them up.

7
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:12,599
And when the lawyer asked it, is

8
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,600
this case real, it said yes.

9
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,339
Last time, we saw that AI

10
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:19,319
guesses what sounds right, not

11
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:19,880
what is right.

12
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:21,860
Here, those two split apart.

13
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:23,940
Why would it do that?

14
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,180
Let's un-jargon it.

15
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:29,147
It wasn't lying.

16
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:31,327
Lying means you know the truth.

17
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:33,646
It was guessing, like a student

18
00:00:33,647 --> 00:00:35,307
bluffing in a closed book exam.

19
00:00:36,447 --> 00:00:37,926
When an AI makes something up

20
00:00:37,927 --> 00:00:39,786
and says it like a fact, that's

21
00:00:39,787 --> 00:00:40,927
called a hallucination.

22
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:42,926
Odd word, because it isn't

23
00:00:42,927 --> 00:00:43,567
seeing things.

24
00:00:44,147 --> 00:00:44,907
It's bluffing.

25
00:00:45,987 --> 00:00:47,686
Picture that student stuck on a

26
00:00:47,687 --> 00:00:48,047
question.

27
00:00:48,847 --> 00:00:50,226
Leave it blank and that's zero

28
00:00:50,227 --> 00:00:50,687
points.

29
00:00:51,287 --> 00:00:52,446
Write something that sounds

30
00:00:52,447 --> 00:00:54,246
right and maybe it gets marked

31
00:00:54,247 --> 00:00:54,627
correct.

32
00:00:55,687 --> 00:00:56,727
So three questions.

33
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:59,046
Why does AI make things up at

34
00:00:59,047 --> 00:00:59,287
all?

35
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:01,107
Why does it sound so sure?

36
00:01:01,887 --> 00:01:03,127
And how do you protect yourself?

37
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:06,232
Question one, why make things up

38
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:06,673
at all?

39
00:01:07,673 --> 00:01:09,013
Because of how it gets marked,

40
00:01:09,673 --> 00:01:12,132
in 2025, researchers at

41
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:14,552
OpenAI, the company behind

42
00:01:14,553 --> 00:01:16,892
ChatGPT, published a paper

43
00:01:16,893 --> 00:01:18,632
called Why Language Models

44
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:19,253
Hallucinate.

45
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:21,293
Their answer is that exam.

46
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:24,093
When AIs are trained and tested,

47
00:01:24,593 --> 00:01:25,932
most of the scoring works the

48
00:01:25,933 --> 00:01:26,473
same way.

49
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:29,252
A right answer gets a point, a

50
00:01:29,253 --> 00:01:31,452
wrong answer gets zero, and I

51
00:01:31,453 --> 00:01:32,953
don't know, also gets zero.

52
00:01:33,493 --> 00:01:35,073
So bluffing always wins.

53
00:01:36,153 --> 00:01:37,272
And some facts it has barely

54
00:01:37,273 --> 00:01:37,733
seen.

55
00:01:38,313 --> 00:01:40,572
The researchers asked one AI for

56
00:01:40,573 --> 00:01:42,272
one of their birthdays and told

57
00:01:42,273 --> 00:01:43,913
it to answer only if it knew.

58
00:01:44,473 --> 00:01:46,532
Three tries, three different

59
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:48,253
dates, all wrong.

60
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:54,472
Question two, why does it sound

61
00:01:54,473 --> 00:01:55,013
so sure?

62
00:01:56,113 --> 00:01:57,612
Remember, it's always guessing

63
00:01:57,613 --> 00:01:58,593
what sounds right.

64
00:01:59,033 --> 00:02:00,772
A real fact and a made up one

65
00:02:00,773 --> 00:02:02,632
come out of the same machine, in

66
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:04,653
the same smooth, friendly voice.

67
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:06,492
There's no wobble when it's

68
00:02:06,493 --> 00:02:06,793
wrong.

69
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:09,232
And its training to be a helpful

70
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:10,732
assistant was marked the same

71
00:02:10,733 --> 00:02:11,073
way.

72
00:02:11,513 --> 00:02:13,332
A sure answer beats, I'm not

73
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:13,653
sure.

74
00:02:14,593 --> 00:02:16,352
That's how an airline's chatbot

75
00:02:16,353 --> 00:02:18,392
in Canada told a man whose

76
00:02:18,393 --> 00:02:20,432
grandmother had just died that

77
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:22,052
he could buy a full price ticket

78
00:02:22,053 --> 00:02:23,972
and claim a family funeral

79
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:24,893
discount later.

80
00:02:25,513 --> 00:02:27,052
The airline's own website said

81
00:02:27,053 --> 00:02:27,533
the opposite.

82
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:31,092
In 2024, a tribunal made the

83
00:02:31,093 --> 00:02:31,853
airline pay.

84
00:02:32,393 --> 00:02:33,713
Its chatbot, its mistake.

85
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:36,506
Question three, how do you

86
00:02:36,507 --> 00:02:37,307
protect yourself?

87
00:02:37,947 --> 00:02:38,587
Three habits.

88
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:41,766
One, ask for sources and then

89
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:42,847
actually open them.

90
00:02:43,347 --> 00:02:44,167
Remember the lawyer?

91
00:02:44,807 --> 00:02:46,506
He asked for cases and got

92
00:02:46,507 --> 00:02:46,967
cases.

93
00:02:47,587 --> 00:02:48,767
They just weren't real.

94
00:02:49,407 --> 00:02:50,426
A link you haven't clicked

95
00:02:50,427 --> 00:02:51,227
proves nothing.

96
00:02:52,107 --> 00:02:54,027
Two, make it an open book exam.

97
00:02:55,007 --> 00:02:56,366
Paste in the real thing, the

98
00:02:56,367 --> 00:02:58,127
letter, the webpage, the rules,

99
00:02:58,487 --> 00:03:00,126
and ask it to answer only from

100
00:03:00,127 --> 00:03:00,407
that.

101
00:03:00,887 --> 00:03:02,586
It guesses far less when the

102
00:03:02,587 --> 00:03:04,086
answer is right in front of it.

103
00:03:04,087 --> 00:03:06,547
Far less, not never.

104
00:03:07,727 --> 00:03:09,366
Three, double check the details

105
00:03:09,367 --> 00:03:10,627
that are easiest to bluff.

106
00:03:11,707 --> 00:03:12,846
Names, numbers, dates, and

107
00:03:12,847 --> 00:03:13,067
quotes.

108
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,620
So why does AI hallucinate?

109
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:18,159
It's a guesser, marked like a

110
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:19,879
student in an exam where a blank

111
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:20,680
scores zero.

112
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:21,920
So it bluffs.

113
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,519
And right answers and wrong ones

114
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:26,039
come out in the same confident

115
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:26,520
voice.

116
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:27,999
So you can't hear the

117
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:28,400
difference.

118
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,159
Sounding sure is not the same as

119
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:31,680
being right.

120
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:34,199
So treat it like a very fast

121
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:35,760
student, not an expert.

122
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,379
Give it the book and check its

123
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:38,660
work.

124
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:41,347
But so far, AI only talks.

125
00:03:41,987 --> 00:03:43,606
What happens when it can act and

126
00:03:43,607 --> 00:03:45,287
book the flight, send the email,

127
00:03:45,527 --> 00:03:46,127
spend the money?

128
00:03:46,707 --> 00:03:48,506
That's called an AI agent, and

129
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:48,987
it's next.

130
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:51,587
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
